content.thinking:
Now I have a very critical observation. Let me look at the test at line 737-742:

```move
let (borrowed_balance, loan) = reserve.borrow_flash_loan<MainMarket, BTC>(amount + 1);

std::unit_test::destroy(ctokens);
std::unit_test::destroy(borrowed_balance);
std::unit_test::destroy(loan);  // ← using std::unit_test::destroy on a hot potato!
std::unit_test::destroy(reserve);
scenario_value.end();
```

This test is using `std::unit_test::destroy(loan)` to destroy the hot potato! This is a test utility, not production code - so in production it can't be abused. 

The real question is whether the `FlashLoan` hot potato can be dropped without repayment in actual production code. In Move, structs without the `drop` ability are enforced by the VM itself at the bytecode level, so if `FlashLoan` lacks `drop`, it's impossible to discard it without calling `repay_flash_loan`.

